other run takes the slot meanwhile, so the total wait never exceeds the builder's cap plus the grace whatever holders come and go; while no live record is there, or none that parses, the line reads `held by unknown` with no end and the provisional bound is the builder's cap plus the grace; a wait that ends unmet exits 123 with `bridge-run: slot not free - held by <kind> <run id> since <start>Z, ends by <end>Z`, naming the holder of the last live record read and its end, or `held by unknown` when no live record was read, a code distinct from the command's limit (124) and the refusals (125), and such a run is never marked healthy; the log entry in `runs.jsonl` records `waited` apart from `seconds`, which is the command's own, so `--limit auto` learns nothing from a wait; (c) `loop-guard.py` knows the `driver` kind (its kind pattern and `KIND_CEILING`) and counts a slot-taking kind's launched command for the heavy launch's one sum — the kind's cap plus the longest wait (the builder's cap, 14,400 s) plus the grace (120 s) plus the waiter's reporting minute — with its 600 s notification grace on top, the same sum step 3's `timeout` uses (TASK-030), the driver's count pinned in `tests/test_loop_guard.py`; (d) `tests/test_bridge_run_slot.py` (names R-040): two heavy runs started together run one after the other and the second's stderr shows the waiting line naming the first's kind, id, start and end, read from the holder's record by a second process while the first still holds the lock (on Windows too), then `slot taken`; a waiter that reads before the record is published prints `held by unknown` and fixes its bound from the record once published; a holder that itself waited before taking the slot gives a waiter whose bound still covers the holder's ceiling; a review takes no slot; a holder killed mid-run frees the slot at once and its record reads as none; a second holder taking the slot during a wait leaves the waiter's bound as first fixed; a wait that outlives its bound ends with exit 123 and its line, the end time on it; `seconds` excludes the wait; the lock's path comes from the home, so the tests' temporary home (`tests/conftest.py`'s `home_of_the_tests`) isolates them from the owner's lock; `tests/test_loop_guard.py` gains the driver kind and the widened count; (e) at the inspect step the supervisor observes one launcher run waiting in the slot for more than thirty minutes, started from the Bash tool with the `timeout` step 3 names, and records under ISS-011, beside KP-053's open question, whether the tool let it reach `slot taken` — the sign of an outside kill being an `.err` holding waiting lines and neither `slot taken` nor `bridge-run: exit`; the command reference regenerated; `windowless-check.py` green; no new dependency.
